 <title>‘A three-minute history of England written in the tour bus toilet’: how Tenpole Tudor made Swords of a Thousand Men</title>
 <link>https://www.theguardian.com/culture/2026/nov/09/tenpole-tudor-made-swords-of-a-thousand-men-king</link>
 <description>&lt;p&gt;‘Dressed as knights, we’d walk onstage to the sound of the William Tell overture. I’d quote Henry V and the crowd would shout, “You’re the rightful king of England!”’&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;I’d worked up the basic idea on Stiff Records’ three-month tour of the northern hemisphere. It’s near-impossible to write songs on the road because you lack the basic solitude, so I’d retire to the tour coach latrine. The melody was really a boogie-blues progression, part-inspired by Norman Greenbaum’s &lt;a href="https://www.theguardian.com/culture/2025/oct/20/norman-greenbaum-spirit-in-the-sky-cook-no-1-jesus"&gt;Spirit in the Sky&lt;/a&gt; and Canned Heat’s On the Road Again. But lyrics only flowed when we came off the tour. Rehearsing near our favourite drinking haunt, &lt;a href="https://www.facebook.com/groups/234553643232745/posts/7389448111076560/"&gt;the Castle&lt;/a&gt; on Holloway Road, London, provided the opening line: “Deep in the castle and back from the war.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.theguardian.com/culture/2026/nov/09/tenpole-tudor-made-swords-of-a-thousand-men-king"&gt;Continue reading...&lt;/a&gt;</description>
